 S.D. and Owen, Bryn M. and Bookout, A.L. and Atkin, S.D. and Wilson, K. and Cotter, S.C. and Finck, B.N. and Satapati, S. and He, T.T. and Burgess, Shawn C. and {Machovsky-Capuska}, G.E. and S{\'a}nchez, J. and Palou, A. and Pic{\'o}, C. and Sarruf, D.A. and Thaler, J.P. and Morton, G.J. and German, J. and Fischer, J.D. and Ogimoto, K. and Wu, L.E. and Melvin, R.G. and Mitchell, S.J. and Coogan, S.C.P. and Simanainen, U.K. and Handelsman, D.J. and Soultoukis, G.A. and Stemmer, K. and Zani, F. and Habegger, Kirk M. and Neff, C. and Kotzbeck, P. and Bauer, M. and Yalamanchilli, S. and Azad, A. and Lehti, M. and Martins, P.J. and Stone, K.P. and Wanders, D. and Orgeron, M. and Cortez, C.C. and Talukdar, Saswata and Song, P. and Hernandez, G. and Zhou, Y. and Scott, W.T. and Paratala, B. and Turner, T. and Smith, A. and Uebanso, T. and Taketani, Y. and Yamamoto, H. and Amo, K. and Ominami, H. and Arai, H. and Takei, Y. and Masuda, M. and Tanimura, A. and Harada, N. and von {Holstein-Rathlou}, S. and BonDurant, L.D. and Peltekian, L. and Yin, T.C. and Claflin, K.E. and Urizar, A.I. and Madsen, A.N. and Ratner, C. and Holst, B. and Xu, J. and Lloyd, D.J. and Hale, C. and Stanislaus, S. and Chen, M. and Sivits, G. and Vonderfecht, S. and Hecht, R. and Lindberg, R.A. and Zhang, X. and Yeung, D.C. and Karpisek, M. and Stejskal, D. and Zhou, Z.G. and Liu, F. and Wong, R.L. and Chow, W.S. and Tso, A.W. and Lam, K.S. and Xu, A. and Xie, Y. and Berglund, E.D. and Coate, K.C. and Katafuchi, T. and Xiao, G. and Wei, W. and Wan, Y. and Zhou, D. and Palam, L.R. and Jiang, L. and Narasimhan, J. and Staschke, K.A. and Wek, R.C.},
  year = {2016},
  month = oct,
  journal = {Cell Metabolism},
  volume = {24},
  number = {4},
  pages = {555--565},
  publisher = {Elsevier Inc.},
  issn = {15504131},
  doi = {10.1016/j.cmet.2016.09.001},
  abstract = {Fibroblast growth factor 21 (FGF21) is the first known endocrine signal activated by protein restriction. Although FGF21 is robustly elevated in low-protein environments, increased FGF21 is also seen in various other contexts such as fasting, overfeeding, ketogenic diets, and high-carbohydrate diets, leaving its nutritional context and physiological role unresolved and controversial. Here, we use the Geometric Framework, a nutritional modeling platform, to help reconcile these apparently conflicting findings in mice confined to one of 25 diets that varied in protein, carbohydrate, and fat content. We show that FGF21 was elevated under low protein intakes and maximally when low protein was coupled with high carbohydrate intakes. Our results explain how elevation of FGF21 occurs both under starvation and hyperphagia, and show that the metabolic outcomes associated with elevated FGF21 depend on the nutritional context, differing according to whether the animal is in a state of under- or overfeeding.},
  pmid = {27693377}
}

